```javascript
function findObjectByName(obj, name) {
  const stack = [{ obj, path: [] }];

  while (stack.length > 0) {
    const { obj, path } = stack.pop();

    if (obj.name === name) {
      return path;
    }

    for (const key in obj) {
      if (typeof obj[key] === 'object' && obj[key] !== null) {
        stack.push({ obj: obj[key], path: [...path, key] });
      }
    }
  }

  return null;
}
```

This function uses a stack data structure to traverse the object hierarchy. It starts with the initial object and pushes it onto the stack along with an empty path. Then, it enters a loop where it pops objects from the stack and checks if their `name` property matches the desired name. If a match is found, it returns the path used to reach the object.

If no match is found after traversing all objects, the function returns `null`.

Note that this implementation assumes that the objects have a `name` property. If your objects have different properties or structures, you may need to modify the code accordingly.

