Here is an example of how you can compute the nth Fibonacci number modulo p in JavaScript:

```javascript
function fibonacciModulo(n, p) {
  if (n === 0) return 0;
  if (n === 1) return 1;

  let prev = 0;
  let curr = 1;

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const next = (prev + curr) % p;
    prev = curr;
    curr = next;
  }

  return curr;
}
```

This function uses a loop to calculate the Fibonacci numbers up to the nth term, taking the modulo p at each step to prevent overflow.

You can call this function with your desired values of n and p like this:

```javascript
const result = fibonacciModulo(10, 5);
console.log(result); // Output: 5
```

